 1 big thing: Surviving chaos 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Jim VandeHei has warned us that we're locked in a new, sustained period of constant, wild, holy-crap changes — good and bad. AI is the instigator. Here are some pointers on how we can survive and thrive, adapted from this weekend's edition of his Axios CEO newsletter: 

 Many of you feel your company is moving quicker than ever, sometimes with dizzying changes and too little explanation. My aim is to take you inside the way heads of organizations are thinking, or should be.

 For leaders, this is a survival guide. And these changes are trickling down to every single employee. So if you're not the boss, I want to help you adapt, contribute and ask better questions. Forget wartime vs. peacetime CEOs. That choice seems quaint.

 Why it matters: Ben Horowitz's famous war vs. peace frame dates to 2011. It assumed companies were in war mode (start-ups or scaling fast) or peace mode (established, maturing) based on circumstance. Truth is, we're all Chaos CEOs or working for Chaos-Era Companies now. 

 Chaos CEO, simplified: Leaders built for the speed of " post-processable velocity " with the smarts to calmly navigate constant, infinite chain reactions . 🔄 A quick refresher: 

 Post-processable velocity = events outrunning our ability to keep up. Infinite chain reactions = politics, AI, media and business blending and immediately bleeding into each other. Most leadership jobs now require the best of both wartime and peacetime skills, as well as brand-new muscles:

 Panoramic vision. Tech, politics, media and markets now collide. We can't understand our company by watching our industry. The unexpected hits come from adjacent spaces. Split-screen vision. We need to see both the whole field panoramically and the next call in front of us. We need to be better at thinking big and small at the same time. Quicker twitch. I told the Axios board our confident line of sight is six weeks, tops. So we need to decide in weeks, not quarters. Move fast on anything reversible. Slow down only for what we can't undo. AI fluency. Old-school CEOs could delegate on tech, both in war and peace. We can't. If we aren't using the models, we can't judge what's now possible. Shift-and-say. Wartime CEOs commit and never blink. Chaos CEOs update just as fast — and publicly — with zero ego about shifting and quickly explaining those adjustments. Context matters, both for informing staff and fine-tuning AI agents. Constant overcommunication soars in importance. Solid core. The more everything moves, the more our people need the two or three things that won't: values, standards and our theory of the case. We need to stress these constantly. Wartime urgency, peacetime calm. The wartime CEO of Horowitz's telling is a bulldozer. That fails now. Our people are already rattled. Chaos outside demands calm inside. Chaos is a ladder. Axios chief revenue officer Jacquelyn Cameron is repurposing this "Game of Thrones" lingo. In the chaos, we can find new opportunities to climb toward. 👀 Behind the scenes: Here's a link to a staff note Jim sent on Friday, "How Axios is staying smart in a scary AI moment" — a good way for any employee to think about these times.

 We love to trade ideas: Let us know what you think, or send us notes you think hit the mark. Just reply to this email, or write us here . Share this item . 

