 6. 💰 NEW: DNC&#x27;s super PAC crackdown 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 In an announcement today , DNC Chair Ken Martin is moving to restrict candidates' ties to super PACs and dark-money groups in the party's 2028 presidential primary — a shift that could shake up the race for the White House, Axios' Holly Otterbein writes. 

 Why it matters: Most of the likely Democratic presidential candidates were expected to rely on super PACs to boost their messaging — and go after other contenders. 🔭 Zoom in: Under rules that the DNC is announcing today, Democratic presidential candidates must disavow any super PACs or dark-money groups that either back them or attack their opponents in ads.

 If presidential hopefuls don't follow the guidelines, the DNC warns, they won't be allowed on the stage at primary debates. A DNC official said the change is backed by progressives, including Sens. Bernie Sanders (I-Vt.) and Elizabeth Warren (D-Mass.), as well as more moderate Dems like Rep. Jason Crow (D-Colo.).

